Threat Hunt — Exfiltration via DNS Tunneling
Hypothesis
A compromised internal host is attempting to exfiltrate sensitive data while bypassing firewall restrictions by using DNS Tunneling
. The hypothesis is that malware is encoding data into DNS queries destined for an attacker-controlled domain
.
Data Source
Sysmon: Event ID 22 (DNS Query) to tie lookups to specific processes
.
Network Traffic: Wireshark/PCAP data showing large volumes of DNS traffic
.
Wireshark Display Filter
// Filter for abnormally long DNS query names
dns.qry.name.len > 50
Logic: Standard DNS queries are usually short; labels exceeding 50 characters often indicate encoded data
.
SPL (Splunk)
index=sysmon EventCode=22 
| eval query_len=len(QueryName) 
| where query_len > 60
| stats count, dc(QueryName) as unique_queries by Image, host
| where unique_queries > 100
Logic: Identifies processes making a high volume of uniquely named, long DNS requests, which is a signature of data being "chunked" and sent out
.
Findings
An internal workstation was found sending 5,000+ unique DNS queries to a .xyz domain in 10 minutes
. The process responsible was a suspicious executable in the %TEMP% folder, confirming a beaconing and exfiltration event
.
False Positives
CDN/Tracking Traffic: Some Content Delivery Networks or ad-trackers use long, complex subdomains for load balancing
.
Antivirus Lookups: Certain AV products perform high-volume cloud-based lookups
.
MITRE ATT&CK
Tactic: Command and Control (TA0011) / Exfiltration (TA0010)
.
Technique: Protocol Tunneling (T1572)
.
Detection Improvement
I/O Graphing: Monitor for sudden "fire hose" spikes in DNS traffic using Wireshark’s I/O graphs
.
DNS Protection: Use a secure DNS resolver (e.g., Quad9) that blocks known malicious domains
.
